course: dictionary
lesson: 3518
title: Fauna and flora
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fauna is a formal word for all the animals living in one place or at one time. Think of it as the animal life of a place. «The fauna of Australia» means the animals found there, such as kangaroos, koalas and wombats.
explain: You will often see «flora and fauna» together. Flora means plants and fauna means animals, so flora and fauna means plants and animals. Park signs and nature reports use this pair.
explain: Fauna is a formal word, so everyday talk usually says «wildlife» or «animals». When you meet fauna in a text, read the words around it. A sentence about a place's animals, often next to a word for plants, gives you the clue.
fact: Fauna means all the animals living in one place or at one time.
fact: Flora means plants, so «flora and fauna» means plants and animals.
fact: Fauna comes from Faunus, a Roman god of the woods, and flora comes from Flora, a Roman goddess of flowers.
fact: In a story, «game» means wild animals hunted for food, which is one kind of fauna.
exercise: «The fauna of Australia includes kangaroos, koalas and wombats.» What does fauna mean here? | The plants growing in Australia | The people who visit Australia | *The animals living in Australia | The houses and roads of Australia
exercise: Park signs often say «flora and fauna». What do the two words mean together? | Shops and houses | Weather and clouds | *Plants and animals | Rocks and rivers
exercise: Which word means the plants of a place? | Romans | *flora | goddess | fauna
test: What does fauna mean? | Rain and wind | Plants of a place | A kind of forest | *All the animals living in one place or at one time
test: Everyday talk says «wildlife» for the animals of a place. Which word is the formal one? | animals | wildlife | *fauna | pets
exam: In «The aboriginal Fauna has joined the aboriginal Flora», which two kinds of living things are named? | Rocks and plants | Birds and fish | Cities and rivers | *Animals and plants
